# Generating code

The `howdy create` commands add a database table to the project you are in,
with as much code around it as you ask for:

- `howdy create controller` writes the table, a record, its database
  operations and a JSON API.
- `howdy create pages` writes the same with HTML pages in place of the JSON
  API: a table of records, and forms to add, edit and delete them.
- `howdy create repo` writes the table, the record and its database
  operations, with no routes.
- `howdy create migration` writes only the table.

Run them from inside a project made with [`howdy new`](/docs/cli/new). The
project must have a database, so they do not work in one created with
`--no-database`. Whether it is SQLite or PostgreSQL is detected from
`gleam.toml`, and the generated column types follow it.

## A controller

```sh
howdy create controller users user name:string age:int
```

The first argument is the plural. It names the folder, the table, the routes
and the module of database operations. The second is the singular, which
names the record module and its type. The rest are the fields, described
below.

This creates the slice `src/<project>/users/`:

- `user.gleam` holds the `User` record, input validation, JSON codecs and
  the database table.
- `users.gleam` holds the database operations: `all`, `find`, `create`,
  `update` and `delete`.
- `users_controller.gleam` holds the routes.

The routes are:

- `GET /api/users` returns every user.
- `GET /api/users/:id` returns one user.
- `POST /api/users` creates a user and answers 201.
- `PUT /api/users/:id` replaces every field and answers 200.
- `DELETE /api/users/:id` deletes a user and answers 204.

The command also writes:

- `migrations/create_users.gleam`, a migration that creates the `users`
  table, numbered one above the highest migration in the project
- `test/<project>/users/users_test.gleam`, tests for the database operations
- `test/<project>/users/users_controller_test.gleam`, tests for the routes

Then it registers the controller in `src/<project>.gleam` and the migration
in `platform/database.gleam`, and runs `gleam format`. The migration runs the
next time the app starts, or when you run `gleam run -m tasks/migrate`.

Names of more than one word use underscores:

```sh
howdy create controller blog_posts blog_post title body:string published:bool
```

## Pages

For pages that people use rather than a JSON API:

```sh
howdy create pages users user name:string age:int
```

`howdy create pages` takes the same arguments and writes the same files,
with `users_pages.gleam` and its test in place of the controller and its
test. The pages are:

- `GET /users` shows every user, in a table.
- `GET /users/new` shows the form for a new user.
- `POST /users` creates a user, then shows it.
- `GET /users/:id` shows one user.
- `GET /users/:id/edit` shows the form to change it.
- `POST /users/:id` updates the user, then shows it.
- `POST /users/:id/delete` deletes the user, then goes back to the table.

The pages are built from [Howdy UI](/docs/ui/components) and rendered on the
server as plain links and forms, so they work without JavaScript. That is
why update and delete are `POST` routes: an HTML form cannot send `PUT` or
`DELETE`. A form that fails validation comes back with the values still
filled in and an error under each field that needs fixing.

The first time you run it, the command also writes two files that every set
of pages shares:

- `src/<project>/platform/pages.gleam` holds the layout, form reading and
  redirects.
- `priv/public/assets/pages.css` holds the styles.

Both are written once and never touched again, so they are yours to edit.
Change the layout there and every set of pages follows.

The pages are registered in `src/<project>.gleam`, and the migration in
`platform/database.gleam`, just as for a controller.

Pass `--api` to get the JSON controller from `howdy create controller` as
well, with the pages at `/users` and the API at `/api/users`:

```sh
howdy create pages blog_posts blog_post title body published:bool --api
```

## A repo

For data without any routes, reached from your own pages or from other
slices:

```sh
howdy create repo users user name:string email:string age:int
```

`howdy create repo` takes the same arguments and writes the same files,
except `users_controller.gleam` and its test. It registers the migration
only.

## A migration

For only the table:

```sh
howdy create migration users name:string email:string age:int
```

This writes `migrations/create_users.gleam`, adds it to the list in
`platform/database.gleam`, and nothing else.

It takes the table name alone, without a singular, so every field needs its
type here. A bare `name` would be mistaken for the singular, and the command
tells you so.

## Fields

Fields are `name:type` pairs. The types are `string`, `int`, `float` and
`bool`. For `create controller`, `create pages` and `create repo` a bare
name is a string, so `title` means `title:string`.

Every record also gets an integer `id`, so do not list one. Give at least
one field, and give each field once.

## Names

The plural, the singular and the field names follow the same rule as project
names: lowercase letters, digits and underscores, starting with a letter.

The plural and the singular must differ, because they name two modules in
one folder. Names that would not compile or would break the generated SQL
are refused with a message saying why: Gleam keywords, SQL keywords, names
of modules the generated code imports, and singulars that would shadow a
built-in Gleam type, such as `list`.

## Your code is safe

Existing files are never overwritten. If any file the command would write
already exists, it stops and writes nothing. The two files shared by every
set of pages are the exception: when they exist they are kept as they are,
and the rest is written.

Registering a controller, pages or a migration means editing
`src/<project>.gleam` and `platform/database.gleam`. If either no longer has
the shape `howdy new` gave it, it is left alone, and the command prints what
to add by hand.

## Flags

- `--api`, for `create pages` only: also create the JSON controller under
  `/api/<plural>`.
- `--plain`: print progress as plain lines instead of animating.
